#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""plan_entity.md step 7e's own gate.

    tools/7e-particle-sound-gate

Launches ONE game, drives it, and prints PASS/FAIL per check with the number
each verdict was reached from. Exit 0 only when every check passed.

7e's gate sentence is

    a particle system attached to a handle follows it; soundscapes change on
    entry.

WHY EACH HALF IS WRITTEN THE WAY IT IS

  THE PARTICLE HALF, and the lane brief's warning about it is exact: *a system
  that draws nothing follows perfectly*. A check that only compared two control
  points would pass for a dead effect, for an effect whose .pcf failed to load,
  and for one whose operators emit nothing -- three failures that look identical
  from the outside. So every follow assertion here is paired with the ACTIVE
  PARTICLE COUNT out of `cs_cl_particle_report`, summed over the collection and
  its children (burning_character is five systems and the parent carries few of
  the particles).

  Two more things are asserted that a naive follow check would miss:

    * CONTROL POINT 1 IS FED. burning_character's "Position on Model Random"
      initializer names control point 1 and four of its five children do too. An
      unfed control point reads (0,0,0) -- the centre of the map -- and the
      engine's fallback for that operator emits every particle AT the point. So
      a flame that followed on point 0 alone would burn correctly at the map
      origin. That is what src/sourcesharp/game/shared/particle_follow_mask.h
      exists to prevent, and this is the live check on it.

    * THE SORT ORIGIN MOVES TOO. It is what the client leaf system sorts and
      culls by; control points that move while the sort origin does not is a
      system that gets culled where it used to be.

  THE SUBJECT IS ss_firesmoke, AND THE FIRST CHOICE FAILED FOR A REASON WORTH
  RECORDING. It was the PLAYER ON FIRE: ss_entityflame parents itself to what it
  burns, so the flame moves when the player does. On the first two runs of this
  gate the server created the flame and burned the player for real -- the damage
  ticked all the way through -- and the CLIENT drew nothing. Measured, not
  guessed:

      debug-api ents ss_entityflame        count 1, index 393
      cs_cl_handle_cross_check all      "ss_entityflame  handle 2b40189 ... agree"
      [SourceSharp/Client] ss_entityflame:   0 lines

  So the native client shell IS there and its MANAGED HALF IS NOT.
  C_StandaloneEntity::EnsureManaged pairs a shell by classname through
  OnStandaloneCreate, and the client's discovered layout list -- 44 of them,
  printed at startup -- does not contain ss_entityflame, while it does contain
  ss_firesmoke. ClientEntityFlame.OnSpawn therefore never runs, and OnSpawn is
  the only thing that starts the flame's particle system. A BURNING ENTITY DRAWS
  NO FIRE IN THIS BUILD. That is a defect this step FOUND and does not own -- it
  is written up in .claude/entity-port/requests/7e.md.

  ss_firesmoke is the same shape and is on the working side of that line: a
  networked standalone entity on the parallel hierarchy, whose client half
  (ClientFiresmoke) calls ParticleSystemStart, and whose control point is driven
  by exactly the same per-frame sweep. It is moved with `ent_keyvalue <it> origin
  <x y z>` by a KNOWN distance, and the check is that the control point moved by
  that same distance -- not merely that it changed.

  THE SOUND HALF WAS A MEASUREMENT OF A GAP AND IS NOW AN ASSERTION -- step
  8e-sound. Step 7e wrote it inverted on purpose, asserting "NO soundscape is
  current at rest", "nothing has pushed one" and "the refusal IS printed",
  because at 325e8f137 both ends of the mechanism were present and there was no
  wire between them. Its own note said what the person who wired it had to do:
  come here and turn each one into an assertion that soundscapes DO change on
  entry. That has happened, and this is what the half asserts now:

    * the machinery still WORKS -- `playsoundscape` starts a named soundscape's
      looping sounds and a second one replaces them. Kept from 7e, because
      without it every clause below would be indistinguishable from a soundscape
      system that is simply broken.

    * A SOUNDSCAPE IS CURRENT where the server chose one, and it is the SAME
      one -- the server's own report and the client's, compared in one breath.
      One report cannot settle this: a client that has latched onto a
      `playsoundscape` answers "current 248 [labs_mp]" perfectly while the
      transport is still missing, which is precisely the state 7e measured.

    * IT CHANGES when the player walks from one env_soundscape to another, on
      BOTH ends. A system that answered its first soundscape forever passes a
      one-shot check and fails this one.

    * A SHELL-LESS ENTITY MAKES A NOISE, and it is heard AT ITS OWN POSITION.
      Step 4b measured the defect with two numbers and both are read here: the
      `EmitSound:` trace, which was ZERO for a shell-less entity and three for
      three shelled func_doors, and the RECIPIENT COUNT beside it, which 4b put
      in that trace because an empty filter is the failure mode of every change
      to how a source position is worked out. The same entity is emitted from
      twice -- once with the player 144 units away and once 2,190 -- so a fix
      that emitted every shell-less sound from one fixed point passes the first
      clause and cannot pass the second.

    * logical.nativeReach IS FLAT ACROSS THE EMIT. Step 2e's invisible-range
      counter moved 24 -> 25 for 4b's one shell-less emit. It is the second,
      independent reading on the same defect, and the one a sound that plays for
      some other reason cannot satisfy.

    * and BOTH printed refusals -- 7e's soundscape line and 4b's shell-less
      emit line -- have STOPPED. Counted after the emits rather than before
      them, because a zero taken before anything was emitted is a check that
      cannot fail.

THE LIMIT THIS HARNESS HAS, stated because a gate that hides its own blind spot
is worse than no gate. Moving the fire with `ent_keyvalue <it> origin <x y z>`
moves the SERVER's copy and NOT the client's -- measured, both sides, in one
run: server 400.0 units, client 0.0. So the follow half prints UNPRV rather than
FAIL: a control point that stayed put is the CORRECT answer for an entity that
stayed put on this side, and printing FAIL for it would file the harness's limits
as the port's defects. The gate is still RED, because an unmet gate is unmet.
What would settle it is one thing: any way to move a particle-owning managed
entity that the client sees. The natural one is ss_entityflame on the player,
and that is exactly what the KNOWN GAP check above is about.

DEBT, with a removal owner: this is step 7e's gate and nothing else reads it,
along with cs_cl_particle_report and cs_cl_soundscape_report. All three go when
tools/live-gate grows particle and soundscape steps, or at step 8c, which folds
the live gates into a `make test` leg.
"""
import math
import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time

# The flags every local game carries (+sv_lan 1 unless STEAM_AUTH=1, +fps_max
